{"title":"Elm Home Security","sub":"Launching a premium plan: how much is really new money?","type":"Product launch","industry":"Consumer technology","geo":"United Kingdom","diff":1,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Cannibalisation · incremental profit","prompt":"Elm sells home security cameras and alarms with a $8-a-month app plan (self-monitoring). It wants to launch a premium plan at $18 a month that includes 24/7 professional monitoring, with a call-out if an alarm goes off. The marketing team forecasts strong demand. The CFO wants to know the true annual profit impact.","clarify":[{"q":"Customers today?","a":"160,000 on the $8 plan, costing about $2 a month each to serve."},{"q":"Premium costs?","a":"About $7 a month per customer in total, including the app, the monitoring centre and call-outs (it replaces the $2 cost of the basic plan)."},{"q":"Forecast?","a":"14% of existing customers upgrade; 30,000 new customers join for the premium plan."},{"q":"Launch costs?","a":"$1.3M one-off; $0.94M a year minimum fee to the monitoring partner."}],"structure":{"root":"What is the premium plan worth to Elm each year?","branches":[{"head":"New customers","items":["Number","Margin per month"]},{"head":"Upgrading customers","items":["Number","Extra margin vs the plan they leave"]},{"head":"Costs","items":["Monitoring partner fee","One-off launch cost"]}]},"hypothesis":"The trap is counting upgraders as if they were new revenue. They already pay $8, so only the difference counts.","questions":[{"q":"Calculate the annual profit impact of the premium plan.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"388.459719pt\" height=\"193.048pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 388.459719 193.048\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 193.048 L 388.459719 193.048 L 388.459719 0 L 0 